  "provider_query": "Python standard library documentation for the venv module",
  "answer": "Official and closely related sources for Python’s standard-library `venv` module:\n\n1. **Library reference (current stable, 3.14)** — `venv` creates lightweight virtual environments on top of a base Python install; documents CLI (`python -m venv`), options (`--system-site-packages`, `--symlinks`, `--clear`, `--upgrade`, `--without-pip`, `--prompt`, `--upgrade-deps`, `--without-scm-ignore-files`), and the `EnvBuilder` API.  \n   https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/venv.html\n\n2. **Library reference (3.x stable index)** — Same module page on the default docs site (currently 3.13.x series).  \n   https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html\n\n3. **Tutorial: Virtual Environments and Packages** — How and why to use `venv`, common `.venv` layout, activation on Windows and Unix, and installing packages with `pip`.  \n   https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/venv.html\n\n4. **Development docs tutorial** — Same tutorial chapter on the in-development docs tree.  \n   https://docs.python.org/dev/tutorial/venv.html\n\n5. **CPython source of the library docs (RST)** — Upstream reStructuredText for the tutorial chapter.  \n   https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/HEAD/Doc/tutorial/venv.rst\n\n6. **CPython implementation** — `Lib/venv/__init__.py`: `EnvBuilder`, CLI, and behavior based on PEP 405.  \n   https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/main/Lib/venv/__init__.py\n\n7. **PEP 405 (design)** — The original proposal for the virtual environment layout and isolation model that `venv` implements (linked from the library docs’ history).  \n   https://peps.python.org/pep-0405/\n\n8. **Practical notes tied to the stdlib tool** — Creating an env with a chosen interpreter (`python3.x -m venv`), and the common Linux case where `python3.x-venv` must be installed separately.  \n   https://www.pythontutorials.net/blog/how-do-i-use-different-python-version-in-venv-from-standard-library-not-virtualenv/\n\n**Quick usage (from the official docs):** `python -m venv .venv`, then activate with `source .venv/bin/activate` (Unix/macOS) or `.venv\\Scripts\\activate` (Windows). Added in Python 3.3.",
